Every patch of the queue answerable to its own switch.

`x87-fast`, `sse-fast`, `simd-fast`, `rep-fast`, `smc-same-value`,
`soft-imm`, `inline-lookup` and `pinned-regs` could each be turned off
and A/B'd; patches 15 (`tb-invalidate-fast`), 16 (`tlb-floor`) and 19
(`tls-hot-paths`) could not, having no property at all. So "every
optimization off" was not a control run -- it cleared eight of eleven,
and the three left in are the ones that sit on TB invalidation, the
softmmu TLB and `notdirty_write`, which is exactly where a display or a
self-modifying guest goes wrong. That cost a wrong conclusion on
2026-09-09: a user turned every switch off, the fault stayed, and the M9
patches were written off as innocent when three of them had never been
out of the picture.

Three accelerator properties, all defaulting on, so nothing changes for a
machine that says nothing:

  * `tb-invalidate-fast` (patch 15) gates all four cuts -- the first
    page's range starting at the write rather than the page boundary, the
    per-page `code_lo`/`code_hi` early-out, the single-page path that
    skips the page collection, and the jump cache not being flushed per
    invalidated CF_PCREL block. Off, each goes back to what upstream
    does.
  * `tlb-floor` (patch 16) chooses between our 4096-entry floor and
    upstream's 64/256 at run time (`tlb_dyn_min_bits`), read per resize
    rather than baked in by the preprocessor, so it takes effect at the
    next flush.
  * `tls-hot-paths` (patch 19) chooses in `notdirty_write` between the
    dirty-bitmap helpers that assume the vCPU's RCU read section and the
    guarded ones that take the lock themselves. Both branches must set
    exactly the same bits, which is the whole claim the patch makes; the
    off branch is the oracle for it. The other half of that patch -- the
    translator reading `tcg_ctx` once per operand instead of per access
    -- is parameter passing with no reachable behaviour and has no
    switch.

A switch must turn off its own patch and no one else's, which took one
reordering to be true: patch 24's `soft_imm_absorbs__locked` is now asked
*before* the `tb-invalidate-fast` branch rather than inside the fast path,
so turning patch 15 off no longer silently takes patch 24 with it.  The
DOS battery is what noticed -- with the three off it was still 13/13
cases right in all four combinations, but `tools/smc-guest-test.py`
failed its own "the soft-imm path was reached" assertion, which is
exactly the check that exists so the battery cannot pass by not testing
anything.

Test: the `optimizations` check in `scripts/test.sh` puts all eleven on
the command line and has our own `qemu-system-i386` accept it (a name
that does not exist is still refused, so acceptance means something), and
the DOS batteries run under `QEMU_TCG_OPTS=tb-invalidate-fast=off,
tlb-floor=off,tls-hot-paths=off` to say the off branches are
architecturally the same machine.

